## Root cause (verified, differs from the report's hypothesis)

The report's "LIKELY CAUSE" does **not** exist in this checkout: there is no `skips` type, no `tskips` field, and `methodTree` is just `{method, root}` (grep for `skips|tskips` over /gin returns nothing; `getValue`'s signature is `getValue(path string, params *Params, skippedNodes *[]skippedNode, unescape bool)`). The real cause is the **stale preallocated per-request scratch slices cached in `Engine.pool`**:

- `Engine.allocateContext()` (gin.go:228-232) sizes the scratch slices once, at context-allocation time: `v := make(Params, 0, engine.maxParams)`, `skippedNodes := make([]skippedNode, 0, engine.maxSections)`; contexts are cached in `engine.pool` (sync.Pool, used by `ServeHTTP`).
- `Engine.addRoute` (gin.go:333-340) grows `engine.maxParams`/`engine.maxSections` when a route is added but **never invalidates the pooled contexts**.
- `Context.reset()` (context.go:107) only re-slices: `*c.params = (*c.params)[:0]` — capacity stays 0 forever.
- In `getValue`: the param branch is guarded by `if params != nil && cap(*params) > 0` (tree.go:498) → params are **silently dropped** (handler runs, 200, all params empty = the exact reported symptom); the catchAll branch has no such guard (tree.go:545-556) → `(*value.params)[:i+1]` **panics** ("slice bounds out of range [:1] with capacity 0"); the skippedNodes push (tree.go:434-436) panics the same way.

So: first request (404) allocates a context with cap 0 and puts it back in the pool; after `addRoute` the same context is reused, and its params/skippedNodes slices are too small.

Why the fix is at the engine, not in `getValue`: `TestTreeInvalidParamsType` (tree_test.go:874-885) deliberately asserts that a zero-capacity params slice must **not** panic ("try to trigger slice bounds out of range with capacity 0"), i.e. the `cap(*params) > 0` guard is intentional. Removing it (grow-on-demand) would make that test panic on `n.path[1:]` for its invalid node. In-repo evidence for the expected behaviour: README "Parameters in path" (params populated), `TestTreeRunDynamicRouting`, and `node.addRoute`'s doc "Not concurrency-safe!" (routes may be added at any time, only not concurrently); nothing documents "register routes before serving".

## Fix (gin.go only, +13 lines, no test files touched)

```go
// resetPool drops the contexts currently kept by the pool. Contexts preallocate
// their params and skippedNodes slices with the maxParams and maxSections values
// known when they are allocated, so contexts allocated before a route was added
// are too small to store the parameters of that route. Dropping them makes sure
// that the next requests get contexts with the right capacity.
func (engine *Engine) resetPool() {
	engine.pool = sync.Pool{New: func() any {
		return engine.allocateContext()
	}}
}
```
plus, in `addRoute`, `engine.resetPool()` after `engine.maxParams = paramsCount` and after `engine.maxSections = sectionsCount` (i.e. the cached contexts are invalidated exactly when the tree's sizing requirement changes). No new locking; `sync.Pool` is only touched where the repo already touches it.

## Verification

- Baseline (before editing): `cd /gin && go test ./...` → all packages ok; pristine-HEAD `go test -race -count=1 ./...` → all ok.
- Temporary repro `/tmp/repro3` (go.mod with `replace github.com/gin-gonic/gin => /gin`, GOFLAGS=-mod=mod GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off, httptest), 7 cases: A params+catchAll route added after 404, B control (route up front), C static route added after miss, D param-only added after miss, E static+param siblings, F live `httptest.NewServer` with runtime registration, G static match with wildcard sibling (exercises skippedNodes capacity).
  - **Without the fix**: A and F panic in `getValue` (catchAll, cap 0); D/E return **200 with empty params** (exactly the reported bug); G panics on the skippedNodes push.
  - **With the fix**: all cases pass, e.g. `code=200 name="john" last_name="smith" wild="/is/super/great" ok=true`, identical to the up-front control case (wildcard form includes the leading slash: `/is/super/great`). Ran 3× → "ALL CHECKS PASSED" each time.
- Final: `gofmt -l .` (empty), `go build ./...` ok, `go vet ./...` ok (no copylocks complaint about the `sync.Pool` literal), `go test ./...` all ok, `go test -race -count=1 ./...` all ok. Also verified my patch alone on a pristine `git archive HEAD` copy: `go test ./...` and `go test -race -count=1 ./...` both green → no pre-existing test regresses.
- No pre-existing test asserts the buggy behaviour; no conflict to report. Cleanup: `/tmp/repro3` and helper copies/patches removed; `/gin` has no untracked files.

## Notes

- Other agents are concurrently editing the same checkout (context.go, errors.go, response_writer.go, utils.go — e.g. FileAttachment quote escaping). My diff is limited to gin.go; those files were not touched by me.
- Known residual limitation (out of scope, matches the repo's stated assumptions): a context that is checked out at the instant a route is registered is returned to the new pool with the old capacity. Adding routes while serving is already documented as not concurrency-safe (`node.addRoute`: "Not concurrency-safe!"), and the reported repro is sequential, which the fix handles deterministically.

